            "full-response": "**Answer:** A: yes\n\n**Explanation:**  \nHigh-risk human papillomaviruses (HPVs) can indeed be detected in human breast milk. While the levels may be low, the virus can be present in breast milk, particularly in the milk of infected mothers. This transmission is possible, though the viral load is typically not high enough to cause significant infection in the infant. Studies have confirmed the presence of certain high-risk HPV types (e.g., 16, 18, 31, 33, 35, 37, 45, 52, 58) in breast milk, though the concentrations are generally lower than those found in genital swabs. The detection of these viruses in breast milk underscores the role of breast milk as a potential vector for HPV transmission, though it is not a primary mode of spread. \n\nThus, the answer is **A: yes**.",
